        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"verona-colei-che-non-si-deve-amare-45ae0a8dbe76cf1b\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":1,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.88,\"analysis\":\"Lora directly avows that she loves Rigo. The declarative present-tense wording and its repetition strongly support T. Its emergence while she is faint, stammering, and answering a practical question gives modest evidence of affective overflow, but those delivery conditions do not establish a strong independent E force. The utterance makes no clear commitment or relational undertaking, so P is unsupported.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The abrupt shift from describing her illness to saying “Ti amo” may be expressive discharge, but it can also be fully understood as an emotionally intense avowal; hence only minimal E support.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Lora directly says “Ti amo” to Rigo during their carriage journey; she repeats the same avowal moments later.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Lora speaks while physically overwhelmed—pale, dizzy, and apparently near fainting—and shortly afterward repeats the words “in a breath.” The narration identifies Rigo as her lover while also calling him her brother, and frames their intimacy through “inexpiable sin” and desperate guilt. This taboo and anguished context intensifies the avowal but does not convert it into a commitment or create an aspect outside T/P/E.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Nulla; ora passa... passa... Ti amo...”\",\"supports\":\"The first-person present-tense declaration directly presents Lora's loving state as true, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately before speaking, Lora falls back against Rigo's shoulder, presses her chest, says she feels ill and dizzy, closes her eyes, and speaks from a wholly pale face.\",\"supports\":\"The avowal arises amid acute affective and bodily disturbance, providing limited—not decisive—support for expressive/reflexive force.\",\"confidence\":0.88},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Ti amo...” ella disse ancora, in un soffio, all’amante pallido.\",\"supports\":\"Her immediate repetition confirms the avowal and modestly reinforces the possibility of affective overflow; repetition and breathy delivery alone do not warrant high E.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The narration calls Rigo both “il fratello” and “all’amante” and concludes that “il cilicio della colpa inconsumabile” covers their desperate flesh.\",\"supports\":\"This establishes the taboo, guilt-laden relationship that contextualizes the declaration without adding performative force to its core.\",\"confidence\":0.96}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the dominant avowal, while a low E score records limited evidence of affective discharge. The taboo relationship, bodily crisis, and guilt are contextual complications rather than missing dimensions of core force.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
